@lafriks good point, just updated the PR by adding a `ErrSSHBuiltinNotSupported` error and check.

It now shows it's not supported when running the builtin server. (and disabled when ssh is disabled)

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1810977/87959014-8e620780-cab2-11ea-8388-27d74d888c1f.png)

I'm still very concerned that allowing users to add principals - essentially usernames - they will accept without some way of asserting that they control these principals is a security hazard.

In particular your current code:

```go
	// Key name of same user cannot be duplicated.
	has, err := sess.
		Where("content = ? AND name = ? AND type = ?", content, name, KeyTypePrincipal).
		Get(new(PublicKey))
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	} else if has {
		return nil, ErrKeyAlreadyExist{0, "", content}
	}
```

Would allow a situation whereby a malicious Eve could racily add principals that Alice owns certificates to before Alice has a chance to add them. Alice would then add her principals - they would be accepted - but when Alice then pushes her changes they go to Eve's repositories. Couple this with PushToCreate and Eve could steal private repositories. 

If you change to use my suggestion then, Eve would simply be denying Alice her principal - which is a little better but far from perfect. However a Trudy could use the information to figure out which principals are in use - I guess that's OK.

The ordering of principal presentation could also be affected - say Alice has two certificates A & B but only has B added as a principal to Gitea. Chuck then adds A as a prinicipal and suddenly Alice is Chuck by accident. 

I don't know how to fix this - you would really need some way of activating the certificate principal to really make this safe.

Either that or you fall back to only accepting principals that match the username or activated email addresses.

